{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read unirank-benchmark-v2 --lines 45'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"unirank-benchmark-v2\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\",\n  \"version\": \"arxiv:2607.19987v2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"Cached section2.3 method descriptions; not an original-method full-paper audit.\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"34ff9c412b35f3118aae377faca5f658d7bffac0b4ef1c12a4eb0c7937372789\",\n  \"total_lines\": 39,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 40,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: Source: https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19987v2\\n2: Version: arxiv:2607.19987v2\\n3: Scope: section2.3 only, original benchmark method descriptions.\\n4: \\n5: 2.3. Representative Models\\n6: 2.3.1. Stacked Unified Interaction\\n7: This paradigm arranges sequential modeling and feature interaction as consecutive modules. The sequence module first encodes the behavioral history, and the feature interaction module then combines this representation with user, target-item, and context features.\\n8: •\\n9: HiFormer (Gui et al., 2023) pools the behavior history with target attention, tokenizes heterogeneous fields and task queries, and applies hierarchical HiFormer blocks to the stacked tokens.\\n10: •\\n11: RankMixer (Zhu et al., 2025) pools the history with target attention, tokenizes concatenated ranking fields into chunks, and alternates parameter-free Token Mixing with per-token MLPs.\\n12: •\\n13: Zenith (Zhang et al., 2026) separately embeds user and item identifiers, pools historical item attributes with target attention, tokenizes context and target attributes, and applies Zenith interaction blocks.\\n14: •\\n15: TokenMixer (Jiang et al., 2026) combines target-aware sequence summaries with global and group tokens, then applies reversible token mixing and per-token feed-forward networks.\\n16: •\\n17: UniMixer (Ha et al., 2026) flattens histories with user and context fields, then chunk-tokenizes them for UniMixing-Lite blocks.\\n18: •\\n19: HeMix (Wang et al., 2026) combines fixed and feature-conditioned queries with low-rank token mixing over recent and earlier histories.\\n20: •\\n21: SSR (Yu et al., 2026) uses target-attention sequence pooling and multi-view competitive sparse filters with dense fusion.\\n22: 2.3.2. Layer-wise Unified Interaction\\n23: This paradigm integrates sequential and non-sequential representations throughout every network layer.\\n24: •\\n25: OneTrans (Zhang et al., 2025) injects sequence and partitioned non-sequential tokens into every block, using mixed attention, a shared sequence FFN, and token-specific FFNs.\\n26: •\\n27: HyFormer (Huang et al., 2026b) encodes the sequence per layer, decodes global queries, and enriches them with non-sequential tokens.\\n28: •\\n29: MixFormer (Huang et al., 2026a) mixes non-sequential queries, uses cross-attention to retrieve information from the updated sequence, and fuses the resulting representations.\\n30: •\\n31: INFNet (Li et al., 2025b) organizes categorical, sequential, and task tokens around a hub for layer-wise aggregation and broadcasting.\\n32: •\\n33: EST (Liu et al., 2026) uses cross-attention to sequence representations and feature-specific feed-forward layers.\\n34: •\\n35: TokenFormer (Zhou et al., 2026) represents fields, histories, and targets as tokens with full interaction and sliding-window Flex Attention.\\n36: •\\n37: LONGER (Chai et al., 2025) encodes local groups with inner Transformers and joins compressed long-term history with recent queries.\\n38: •\\n39: UltraHSTU (Ding et al., 2026) extends HSTU-style sequential transduction (Zhai et al., 2024) with hybrid attention that combines sparse and full attention in each interaction layer.\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 307 --lines 60'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 307,\n  \"next_line\": 367,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"307: Let αm​j=max(𝒙,y)∈ℬm⁡I⁡(𝒙j≠0)\\\\alpha_{mj}=\\\\max_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}I(\\\\bm{x}_{j}\\\\neq 0)\\n308: denote if there is at least one instance having the feature id jj in mini-batch ℬm\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}.\\n309: Then Eq.(5) can be approximated by\\n310: (6)\\n311: L2​(𝐖)≈∑j=1K∑m=1Bαm​jnj​‖𝒘j‖22.L_{2}(\\\\bm{\\\\mathrm{W}})\\\\approx\\\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\\\sum_{m=1}^{B}\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\|\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\|_{2}^{2}.\\n312: In this way, we derive an approximated mini-batch aware version of ℓ2\\\\ell_{2} regularization.\\n313: For the mm-th mini-batch, the gradient w.r.t. the embedding weights of feature jj is\\n314: (7)\\n315: 𝒘j←𝒘j−η⁡[1|ℬm|​∑(𝒙,y)∈ℬm∂L⁡(p⁡(𝒙),y)∂𝒘j+λ​αm​jnj​𝒘j],\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\leftarrow\\\\bm{w}_{j}-\\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{|\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{B}_{m}}{\\\\frac{\\\\partial L(p(\\\\bm{x}),y)}{\\\\partial\\\\bm{w}_{j}}+\\\\lambda\\\\frac{\\\\alpha_{mj}}{n_{j}}\\\\bm{w}_{j}}\\\\right],\\n316: in which only parameters of features appearing in mm-th mini-batch participate in the computation of regularization.\\n317: 5.2. Data Adaptive Activation Function\\n318: PReLU (He\\n319: et al., 2015) is a commonly used activation function\\n320: (8)\\n321: f⁡(s)={s if ​s>0α​s if ​s≤0.=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,f(s)=\\\\begin{cases}s&\\\\text{ if }s>0\\\\\\\\\\n322: \\\\alpha s&\\\\text{ if }s\\\\leq 0.\\\\end{cases}=\\\\penalty\\\\ p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\n323: where ss is one dimension of the input of activation function f⁡(⋅)f(\\\\cdot) and p⁡(s)=I⁡(s>0)p(s)=I(s>0) is an indicator function which controls f⁡(s)f(s) to switch between two channels of f⁡(s)=sf(s)=s and f⁡(s)=α​sf(s)=\\\\alpha s. α\\\\alpha in the second channel is a learning parameter.\\n324: Here we refer to p⁡(s)p(s) as the control function. The left part of Fig.3 plots the control function of PReLU.\\n325: PReLU takes a hard rectified point with value of 00, which may be not suitable when the inputs of each layer follow different distributions.\\n326: Take this into consideration, we design a novel data adaptive activation function named Dice,\\n327: (9)\\n328: f⁡(s)=p⁡(s)⋅s+(1−p⁡(s))⋅α​s,p⁡(s)=11+e−s−E⁡[s]V​a​r​[s]+ϵf(s)=p(s)\\\\cdot s+(1-p(s))\\\\cdot\\\\alpha s,\\\\ \\\\ p(s)=\\\\frac{1}{1+e^{-\\\\frac{s-E[s]}{\\\\sqrt{Var[s]+\\\\epsilon}}}}\\n329: with the control function to be plotted in the right part of Fig.3.\\n330: In the training phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is the mean and variance of input in each mini-batch.\\n331: In the testing phrase, E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] is calculated by moving averages E⁡[s]E[s] and V​a​r​[s]Var[s] over data.\\n332: ϵ\\\\epsilon is a small constant which is set to be 10−810^{-8} in our practice.\\n333: Dice can be viewed as a generalization of PReLu.\\n334: The key idea of Dice is to adaptively adjust the rectified point according to distribution of input data, whose value is set to be the mean of input.\\n335: Besides, Dice controls smoothly to switch between the two channels.\\n336: When E⁡(s)=0E(s)=0 and V​a​r​[s]=0Var[s]=0, Dice degenerates into PReLU.\\n337: 6. Experiments\\n338: In this section, we present our experiments in detail, including datasets, evaluation metric, experimental setup, model comparison and the corresponding analysis.\\n339: Experiments on two public datasets with user behaviors as well as a dataset collected from the display advertising system in Alibaba demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approach which outperforms state-of-the-art methods on the CTR prediction task.\\n340: Both the public datasets and experiment codes are made available1.\\n341: 6.1. Datasets and Experimental Setup\\n342: Amazon Dataset22\\n343: 2\\n344: http://jmcauley.ucsd.edu/data/amazon/.\\n345: Amazon Dataset contains product reviews and metadata from Amazon, which is used as benchmark dataset(He and McAuley, 2016; Mcauley\\n346: et al., ; Veit\\n347: et al., 2015). We conduct experiments on a subset named Electronics, which contains 192,403 users, 63,001 goods, 801 categories and 1,689,188 samples. User behaviors in this dataset are rich, with more than 5 reviews for each users and goods. Features include goods_id, cate_id, user reviewed goods_id_list and cate_id_list. Let all behaviors of a user be (b1,b2,…,bk,…,bnb_{1},b_{2},\\\\ldots,b_{k},\\\\ldots,b_{n}), the task is to predict the (k+1)-th reviewed goods by making use of the first k reviewed goods.\\n348: Training dataset is generated with k=1,2,…,n−2k=1,2,\\\\ldots,n-2 for each user. In the test set, we predict the last one given the first n−1n-1 reviewed goods.\\n349: For all models, we use SGD as the optimizer with exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 1 and decay rate is set to 0.1. The mini-batch size is set to be 3232.\\n350: MovieLens Dataset33\\n351: 3\\n352: https://grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/20m/. MovieLens data(Harper and\\n353: Konstan, 2015) contains 138,493 users, 27,278 movies, 21 categories and 20,000,263 samples. To make it suitable for CTR prediction task, we transform it into a binary classification data. Original user rating of the movies is continuous value ranging from 0 to 5. We label the samples with rating of 4 and 5 to be positive and the rest to be negative. We segment the data into training and testing dataset based on userID. Among all 138,493 users, of which 100,000 are randomly selected into training set (about 14,470,000 samples) and the rest 38,493 into the test set (about 5,530,000 samples). The task is to predict whether user will rate a given movie to be above 3(positive label) based on historical behaviors. Features include movie_id, movie_cate_id and user rated movie_id_list, movie_cate_id_list. We use the same optimizer, learning rate and mini-batch size as described on Amazon Dataset.\\n354: Alibaba Dataset. We collected traffic logs from the online display advertising system in Alibaba, of which two weeks’ samples are used for training and samples of the following day for testing. The size of training and testing set is about 2 billions and 0.14 billion respectively. For all the deep models, the dimensionality of embedding vector is 12 for the whole 16 groups of features. Layers of MLP is set by 192×200×80×2192\\\\times 200\\\\times 80\\\\times 2. Due to the huge size of data, we set the mini-batch size to be 5000 and use Adam(Kingma and Ba, 2015) as the optimizer. We apply exponential decay, in which learning rate starts at 0.001 and decay rate is set to 0.9.\\n355: The statistics of all the above datasets is shown in Table 2.\\n356: Volume of Alibaba Dataset is much larger than both Amazon and MovieLens, which brings more challenges.\\n357: Table 2. Statistics of datasets used in this paper.\\n358: Dataset\\n359: Users\\n360: Goodsa\\n361: Categories\\n362: Samples\\n363: Amazon(Electro).\\n364: 192,403\\n365: 63,001\\n366: 801\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
